# Create MATE Ticket

## Arguments

```
[brief description of the ticket idea]
```

## Instructions

1. **Find next ID**

```sh
ls ~/apps/robsonscheffer/usemate/docs/plans/active/ ~/apps/robsonscheffer/usemate/docs/plans/done/ \
  | grep -oE 'MATE-[0-9]+' | sort -t- -k2 -n | tail -1
```

Increment by 1 → `MATE-NNN`.

2. **Derive slug** from the description: lowercase, hyphens, ≤5 words. e.g. `add-stdio-mcp-support`.

3. **Write the ticket** to `~/apps/robsonscheffer/usemate/docs/plans/active/MATE-NNN-<slug>/README.md`:

```markdown
---
id: MATE-NNN
status: open
needs: spec
created: YYYY-MM-DD
updated: YYYY-MM-DD
tags: []
epic: ad-hoc
---

# MATE-NNN — <Title>

## What

<1–3 sentences: what the ticket delivers>

## Why

<1–2 sentences: why it matters>

## Log

- YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM | CREATED | ticket seeded
```

Set `needs: spec` when the description is thin. Set `needs: plan` when What/Why are already clear from the arguments.

4. **Commit immediately** — a ticket left uncommitted blocks the next craft workflow (dirty tree):

```sh
cd ~/apps/robsonscheffer/usemate
git add docs/plans/active/MATE-NNN-<slug>/README.md
git commit -m "chore: add MATE-NNN ticket — <slug>"
```

Run from the bare repo — git operates on the working tree correctly from there.

5. **Report** the ticket ID, path, and status/needs pair chosen.

## Notes

- Legal `(status, needs)` pairs for new tickets: `(open, spec)`, `(open, plan)`, `(open, research)`.
- `topic:` field is optional — only needed when the pipeline resolver needs an alias.
- Full contract: `~/apps/robsonscheffer/usemate/docs/plans/.ticket-contract.yaml`.
